# Error Handling

Never show raw errors, stack traces, or technical details to users. All errors surface through a friendly UI.

## Frontend: React Error Boundary

Wrap the app in an error boundary that catches unexpected render errors and shows a full-page fallback.

```tsx
// ✅ Error boundary catches crashes
<ErrorBoundary>
  <Dashboard />
</ErrorBoundary>

// ❌ Never let errors bubble to a white screen or console dump
```

The fallback UI:
- Centered card with an icon
- Heading: "Something went wrong"
- Body: "We're sorry — something unexpected happened. Please try again later."
- A "Go home" button that navigates to `/`
- No stack trace, no error code, no technical jargon

## Frontend: Async Operations

All async operations (fetches, mutations) must be wrapped in try/catch. On failure, show a toast — never `console.error` and move on silently, and never show the raw error message.

```tsx
// ✅
try {
  await submitCrawl(url);
} catch {
  toast.error("Something went wrong. Please try again later.");
}

// ❌ Exposing raw error
try {
  await submitCrawl(url);
} catch (err) {
  toast.error(err.message);
}

// ❌ Silent failure
try {
  await submitCrawl(url);
} catch (err) {
  console.error(err);
}
```

## API Routes: Response Format

API routes return structured JSON errors with appropriate HTTP status codes. The `error` field is always a user-safe message — log the real error server-side.

```tsx
// ✅ API route error handling
try {
  const result = await doSomething();
  return Response.json(result);
} catch (err) {
  console.error("doSomething failed:", err);
  return Response.json(
    { error: "Something went wrong. Please try again later." },
    { status: 500 }
  );
}
```

Use specific status codes with safe messages:

| Status | When                        | Message                                              |
| ------ | --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| 400    | Validation failure          | Describe what's wrong (e.g. "Please enter a valid URL") |
| 404    | Resource not found          | "The requested resource was not found."              |
| 409    | Conflict                    | "This resource already exists."                      |
| 500    | Unexpected server error     | "Something went wrong. Please try again later."      |

400-level errors may include a specific user-facing message since the user can fix them. 500-level errors must always use the generic message.

## Frontend: API Response Handling

When calling API routes, check the response status and extract the safe error message.

```tsx
// ✅
const res = await fetch("/api/crawl", { method: "POST", body });
if (!res.ok) {
  const { error } = await res.json();
  toast.error(error);
  return;
}

// ❌ Showing status codes or response text to users
toast.error(`Error ${res.status}: ${res.statusText}`);
```
